  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, northerly flow is causing lake effect \nsnow to continue along the South Shore, and this activity is \nexpected to persist into tonight. Elsewhere across the \nNorthland, expect mostly dry and cooler conditions with high \ntemperatures hovering in the 30s. The spotty snow showers across\nnortheast Minnesota from earlier have largely dissipated as \ndrier air begins to work its way into the region. Total \naccumulations along the South Shore will remain light, generally\nan additional dusting to an inch, though a localized two inches\nis possible in the higher terrain. However, this may be hard to\ncapture due to compaction and melting. \n\nHeading into Friday, the lake effect snow over the South Shore will\neventually dissipate due to incoming warmer, drier air. As this\ndrier air mass fully establishes itself over the area, skies will\nbecome mostly sunny for the vast majority of our region. High\ntemperatures on Friday will again remain chilly, topping out in the\nmiddle to upper 30s with light northerly winds settling in across\nthe Northland.\n\nAs a ridge moves over us for the rest of the weekend, temperatures\nwill warm again into the 40s. High pressure moving into the Great\nLakes region will provide plenty of sunshine and dry weather for\nboth Saturday and Sunday. Winds will become light and variable on\nSaturday before shifting to a southerly direction by Sunday, helping\nassist with the slow and steady warming trend across the Arrowhead\nand northwest Wisconsin.\n\nThe upper level ridge will remain firmly in place through early next\nwork week, allowing temperatures to climb even further into the 50s\nby Monday and Tuesday. Widespread dry weather will persist, with low\ntemperatures generally falling into the 20s and 30s at night.\nBreezier southerly winds will develop during this timeframe, but\nconditions will remain quiet overall.\n\nGlobal models are still in agreement of a larger system akin to a\nColorado low for late next week to bring the next round of\nprecipitation. From how temperature trends look for this system, the\nsole form of precipitation expected will be rain. This system is\nexpected to track into the Northern Plains and Upper Midwest in the\nThursday and Friday timeframe, bringing widespread soaking rainfall\nto the region.",
